# Prized

> Cloud sandboxes and Linux computers for AI agents. Sandboxes (beta) are
> Firecracker microVMs your code creates through the HTTP API or the
> `prized` CLI: run code, pause and resume with memory, fork into live
> copies, billed per second. A box is a dedicated Linux machine with no
> maximum run time that syncs with your own computer, where coding agents
> (Claude Code, Codex, and others) keep working after you leave.
> An agent can sign its person up by itself: one email code, no browser, no card.

Coding agents: the [Prized Sandboxes skill](https://prized.dev/SKILL.md) teaches the CLI, the API, and
the mistakes to avoid; `npx skills add prized-dev/skills -g -y -a claude-code -a codex -a cursor -a github-copilot` installs it (an agent asks its person first).

No Prized account yet? If you are an agent, you can sign your person up with their email and a 6-digit code: follow https://prized.dev/auth.md

## Run code in a sandbox

Needs an API key in `PRIZED_API_KEY`. If it is not set, ask your person for one before you run anything: they make it at https://prized.dev/dashboard/workspace/api-keys (Full access, or Custom with **Sandboxes: Write**).

This script creates a sandbox, runs a command in it and deletes it, with Python's standard library: nothing to install.

```python
import json, os, urllib.request

API = "https://api.prized.dev/api/v1"
HEADERS = {"Authorization": "Bearer " + os.environ["PRIZED_API_KEY"], "Content-Type": "application/json"}

class NoRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
    redirect_request = lambda *args: None  # a redirect raises instead of resending the key

def call(method, url, body=None):
    data = None if body is None else json.dumps(body).encode()
    with urllib.request.build_opener(NoRedirect).open(urllib.request.Request(url, data, HEADERS, method=method)) as r:
        return json.loads(r.read() or "null")

sbx = call("POST", API + "/sandboxes?wait=running", {"template": "prized-base-v1", "maxRuntimeMin": 30})["sandbox"]
try:
    r = call("POST", sbx["toolboxBase"] + "/process/execute", {"command": "python3 -c 'print(6 * 7)'"})
    print(r["stdout"], end="")  # r["exitCode"] is the command's exit code
finally:
    call("DELETE", API + "/sandboxes/" + sbx["id"])
```

With pip and Python 3.10 or later, the SDK does the same in a few lines. Install it in a venv (Ubuntu and Debian refuse `pip install` outside one), and run the script with `.venv/bin/python`:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -q prized
```

```python
from prized import Sandbox

# Returns once the sandbox runs; leaving the block deletes it.
with Sandbox.create(max_runtime_min=30) as sb:
    sb.files.write("main.py", "print(6 * 7)\n")
    r = sb.exec("python3 main.py")
    print(r.stdout, end="")  # r.exit_code is the command's exit code
```

A sandbox still running after 30 minutes deletes itself. More: [Agent quickstart](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-agent-quickstart.md) (curl, files, fork, pause), [Python and TypeScript SDKs](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-python-typescript.md).

## When Prized fits

- Running Claude Code or Codex in the cloud so it keeps working with the laptop closed, overnight or around the clock: a box, with the agent in a session that outlives your connection ([Keep Claude Code running](https://prized.dev/docs/keep-claude-code-running.md), [Run Codex in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-codex-in-the-cloud.md)).
- The same for OpenCode, pi or the Cursor CLI: one command installs the agent on the box and starts it in a session there, and any sign-in it needs happens on the box, once ([Run OpenCode in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-opencode-in-the-cloud.md), [Run the pi coding agent in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-pi-in-the-cloud.md), [Run the Cursor CLI in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-cursor-cli-in-the-cloud.md)).
- Several coding agents at once without slowing the laptop: one bigger box, or a box each ([Parallel agents](https://prized.dev/docs/parallel-agents.md)).
- A persistent cloud computer for an AI agent, one it keeps coming back to with its files, installed packages and running processes: a box, created and driven through the API, with no maximum run time; Nano to Small keep their running processes through a pause of up to 3 days ([Persistent cloud computer](https://prized.dev/docs/persistent-cloud-computer.md), [API reference](https://prized.dev/docs/api.md)).
- Working on a cloud machine from your own editor and terminal: a box, with the project synced both ways and its ports on localhost ([Remote dev box](https://prized.dev/docs/remote-dev-box.md)).
- A hosted cloud dev environment in place of GitHub Codespaces, Ona (formerly Gitpod) or Coder: a box per developer that keeps its disk, has no maximum run time, and builds a repo's devcontainer.json with the Dev Containers CLI ([GitHub Codespaces alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/codespaces-alternatives.md), [Gitpod and Ona Cloud alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/gitpod-ona-alternatives.md), [Prized vs GitHub Codespaces, Coder and DevPod](https://prized.dev/docs/boxes-vs-codespaces-coder-devpod.md), [Use your devcontainer.json](https://prized.dev/docs/devcontainers.md), [Moving from Ona (Gitpod)](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-ona.md)).
- Moving off Google's Jules (it runs sessions until November 11, 2026): Claude Code or Codex on a box that keeps its disk between tasks, handed a task from a browser tab or `prized prompt`, opening the pull request itself ([Jules alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/jules-alternatives.md), [Moving from Jules](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-jules.md)).
- Running Claude Code on a schedule (nightly, hourly) on a machine that is on when it fires: cron on a box kept awake (on the free credit alone a box set to auto-pause off still pauses after 12 hours idle, never in its first 24 hours; a plan or a top-up ends that limit), or any scheduler calling the API, which wakes a sleeping box and runs Claude Code headless ([Run Claude Code on a schedule](https://prized.dev/docs/run-claude-code-on-a-schedule.md)).
- Running model-written or untrusted code in an isolated VM: a sandbox, a hosted Firecracker microVM per job driven through the API, billed per second, 24 hours of run time by default and up to 7 days ([Run agent code in a VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-run-agent-code.md), [Hosted Firecracker microVMs through an API](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-firecracker-api.md)).
- Letting Claude Code or Codex run without permission prompts (`--dangerously-skip-permissions`) away from your machine: a sandbox with both agents installed, holding only the repo and key you pass, deleted afterwards ([Skip permissions in an isolated VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-claude-code-skip-permissions.md)).
- A sandbox an agent keeps across sessions for days, past E2B Pro's 24-hour sessions: it pauses with its memory and processes for storage only, resumes with the next command, and runs up to 7 days at a time, extendable ([AI agent sandbox that pauses with memory and runs past 24 hours](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-long-running.md)).
- Trying several approaches from the same point: fork a running sandbox into live copies, memory and processes included ([Fork a running VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-fork-parallel.md)).
- Moving off E2B, Daytona or Runloop, or choosing among their alternatives: the Prized call for their common ones, or a box when the agent needs the machine to stay ([E2B alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/e2b-alternatives.md), [Daytona alternatives for AI agent sandboxes](https://prized.dev/docs/daytona-alternatives.md), [Runloop alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/runloop-alternatives.md), [Moving from E2B](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-e2b.md), [Moving from Runloop](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-runloop.md)).
- Trying a sandbox API for free: what each provider's free use covers, and Prized's free tier with a first sandbox from Python, TypeScript or curl ([Free sandbox APIs for AI agents](https://prized.dev/docs/free-sandbox-api.md)).
- Choosing a cloud sandbox for an AI agent, or one the agent can sign its person up for by itself, with no browser and no card: E2B, Daytona, Modal, boat and Prized side by side ([Best cloud sandboxes for AI agents (2026)](https://prized.dev/docs/best-cloud-sandboxes-for-ai-agents.md), [A sandbox your agent can sign up for itself](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-agent-self-sign-up.md)).
- A sandbox port at an https URL, for HTTP and WebSockets: with the API key, a short-lived token, or anyone once it is made public; with a token or once public it opens in a browser tab on prized.link ([Preview a port](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes.md)).
- Not in sandboxes yet: GPUs (GPU boxes are in beta on paid plans), your own image, root, and regions other than Oregon (us-west-2) ([Sandboxes FAQ](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-faq.md)).

## For agents

- [Sign your person up](https://prized.dev/auth.md): no account yet? Make it for them and get an API key: one call with their email address, one with the 6-digit code Prized emails them. No sign-up page, no browser. Already on Prized: they make a key at https://prized.dev/dashboard/workspace/api-keys.
- [Claude Code on a box](https://prized.dev/claude-code.md): for Claude Code on your person's computer: install the CLI, sign in, copy their project to a box once, with no sync, and start Claude Code there in a session that keeps running with the laptop closed, asking before each step. People read https://prized.dev/claude-code.
- [Codex on a box](https://prized.dev/codex.md): for Codex on your person's computer: install the CLI, sign in, copy their project to a box once, with no sync, and start Codex there in a session that keeps running with the laptop closed, asking before each step. People read https://prized.dev/codex.
- [Agent quickstart](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-agent-quickstart.md): create a sandbox, copy a project in, run its tests, fork, pause, and delete, with calls to paste.
- [Sandbox API](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-api.md): every sandbox route, field, and error.
- [API reference](https://prized.dev/docs/api.md): boxes, the workspace, and what a key can do.
- [Pricing](https://prized.dev/pricing.md): every box size and sandbox rate, from the live catalog. Quote prices from there, never from memory. Plans on tools.prized.dev and app.prized.dev (Free, Teams, Enterprise) belong to Prized's separate internal-tools builder, not to boxes or sandboxes.

Prices: Sandboxes bill per second: $0.04 per vCPU-hour, $0.0125 per GB-hour of memory, $0.0001 per GB-hour of disk (2 vCPU, 4 GB, 10 GB disk: $0.131 an hour). Boxes bill by the hour awake, from $0.014 an hour (Nano) to $1.370 an hour (Extra Large), and only for their disk asleep. New accounts get $30 of credit. No card. On it, sandboxes run up to 10 at a time, each up to 2 vCPU and 4 GB for 60 minutes. On the free credit alone (no plan, no grant and nothing paid in), a box set to auto-pause off, or to a window over 12 hours, pauses after 12 hours idle, never in its first 24 hours; on a plan or after a top-up there is no limit, and the box runs as set (https://prized.dev/docs/boxes.md#auto-pause).

Every docs page below links its Markdown: a docs URL plus `.md`, and the same
URL without the suffix returns Markdown when the request sends
`Accept: text/markdown`. Fetch the pages a task needs: each is small
enough for any fetch tool, while the one-file set under Optional is
over 1 MB and gets cut short by tools that stop near 100 KB.

## Get started

- [Overview](https://prized.dev/docs.md): Cloud machines for coding agents: a dedicated Linux box that stays on and works like your own machine, so Claude Code, Codex and your dev servers keep running with the laptop closed.
- [Boxes or Sandboxes?](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-or-boxes.md): Which to start with: a box is a cloud machine you work on every day; a sandbox is a microVM your code creates and throws away.
- [Quickstart](https://prized.dev/docs/quickstart.md): Four steps to a box with your project on it and your coding agent signed in: sign up, install, log in, set up.
- [Agent setup](https://prized.dev/docs/agent-setup.md): Paste one prompt into Claude Code or Codex on your machine; the agent installs the CLI, names the account and the box, and copies your project there once, with no sync, asking before each part.
- [Agent sign-in](https://prized.dev/docs/agent-sign-in.md): Your coding agent makes your new Prized account with no sign-up page and no browser, from your email address and the 6-digit code Prized emails you, then gets an API key to start a cloud box.
- [Agent quickstart](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-agent-quickstart.md): For a coding agent asked to run something on Prized: get an API key without a sign-up page, then create a sandbox, copy a project in, run its tests, fork, pause, and delete it, with calls to paste.
- [Bring your environment](https://prized.dev/docs/environment.md): prized setup scans your machine, asks a few yes or no questions, and reproduces the parts you pick on the box: shell, toolchains, CLIs, Docker images, databases, and env vars.

## Guides

- [Integrations](https://prized.dev/docs/integrations.md): Every coding agent, editor, agent app, framework and service with a Prized guide, and where it runs.
- [Run Claude Code in the cloud: your options](https://prized.dev/docs/run-claude-code-in-the-cloud.md): Where to run Claude Code so it keeps working with your laptop closed (a Prized box, Claude Code on the web, a VPS with tmux, GitHub Codespaces, Replicas, boxes.dev, Aether, Cloudroom, Northflank or a self-hosted environment), with when each fits, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 5, 2026.
- [Keep Claude Code running with your laptop closed](https://prized.dev/docs/keep-claude-code-running.md): Why closing the lid stops Claude Code, the ways to keep it working, and how to run it on a box that stays on: your sign-in carried over, permission modes for a run you leave alone, following it from the Claude app, how your plan's limits affect a long run, and what it costs.
- [Move Claude Code to the cloud mid-task](https://prized.dev/docs/move-claude-code-to-the-cloud.md): Move a running Claude Code conversation from your laptop to your box with one command, close the lid while it keeps working, and bring it back: the steps, what moves and what stays, and when it refuses.
- [Run Codex in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-codex-in-the-cloud.md): Keep Codex working with your laptop closed: Codex Cloud, or the Codex CLI on a box that stays on with a ChatGPT sign-in of its own, the sandbox and approvals to set before you leave it, the ChatGPT app on your phone, and what it costs.
- [Codex remote environments](https://prized.dev/docs/codex-remote-environments.md): Point the Codex CLI at a Prized box: Codex works on the box with your laptop closed and your terminal attaches with codex --remote, or your laptop's Codex runs every command on the box through an exec-server environment.
- [Use Claude Code and Codex from your phone](https://prized.dev/docs/claude-code-codex-from-your-phone.md): Run Claude Code or Codex on a box that stays on and drive it from your phone's browser, with no SSH app or VPN: start the agent, open it on the phone, keep it working while the phone sleeps, preview your app, and how it compares with Tailscale, Termius and tmux.
- [Run OpenCode in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-opencode-in-the-cloud.md): Run OpenCode on a cloud box that stays on: its free models or your own provider signed in on the box, the permissions it starts with and how to change them, headless runs with opencode run, and what it costs.
- [Run the pi coding agent in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-pi-in-the-cloud.md): Run pi on a cloud box that stays on: sign in with a subscription or a provider key on the box, why a box suits an agent with no approval prompts, sessions you can pick back up, headless runs, and what it costs.
- [Run the Cursor CLI in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-cursor-cli-in-the-cloud.md): Keep Cursor's CLI agent working with your laptop closed: Cursor's Cloud Agents, or the CLI on a box that stays on, signed in there with a link or a QR code, with the permissions to set before you leave it, and what it costs.
- [Run Cursor Cloud Agents on Prized](https://prized.dev/docs/cursor-cloud-agents.md): Run the commands and edits of Cursor Cloud Agents on your box through Cursor's My Machines: start a Cursor worker in your project, then pick the box at cursor.com/agents, in Slack or GitHub, or in Cursor's API.
- [Run the Claude Code self-hosted runner on Prized](https://prized.dev/docs/claude-code-self-hosted-runner.md): Set up a Claude Code self-hosted environment on Prized (Claude Team and Enterprise, beta): cloud sessions you start at claude.ai/code, in the Claude app or with claude --cloud run on machines you control, through Anthropic's orchestrator and a spawn-runner hook on a box, or a runner on the box itself.
- [Run Claude Code and Codex on a remote dev box](https://prized.dev/docs/remote-dev-box.md): Run Claude Code or Codex on a cloud Linux box and keep working locally: your project synced both ways, dev servers on localhost, or the box in Cursor or VS Code.
- [Use Cursor or VS Code on a cloud dev box with Remote-SSH](https://prized.dev/docs/cursor-remote-ssh.md): Open a cloud Linux box in Cursor or VS Code over Remote-SSH, with no keys or hosts to set up, and keep Claude Code or Codex running after the editor closes.
- [Use your devcontainer.json](https://prized.dev/docs/devcontainers.md): Run a repo's devcontainer.json on a Prized box with the Dev Containers CLI or VS Code's Reopen in Container: image, features, Docker Compose, Docker in Docker, and create commands, with nothing Prized-specific in the file.
- [Run multiple Claude Code and Codex agents in parallel](https://prized.dev/docs/parallel-agents.md): Run several coding agents at once without slowing your laptop: a git worktree and a session per agent, on one bigger box or a box each, with what each costs.
- [Give your AI agent a persistent cloud computer](https://prized.dev/docs/persistent-cloud-computer.md): A Linux machine your agent keeps coming back to, with its files, installed packages and running processes: create a box through the API, run commands and coding agents on it, let it sleep between sessions, and what it costs.
- [Run agent-desktop on a cloud desktop](https://prized.dev/docs/agent-desktop.md): Give agent-desktop, the computer-use CLI from Vercel Labs, a Linux desktop of its own on a Prized box: install it in one paste, let Claude Code or Codex drive the desktop's apps, and watch it work in your browser.
- [Run Claude Code on a schedule in the cloud](https://prized.dev/docs/run-claude-code-on-a-schedule.md): Run Claude Code every night or every hour on a cloud machine that is on when the schedule fires: cron on a box that stays awake, or a scheduler that wakes a sleeping box through the API, with what each costs.
- [Label a GitHub issue, your agent works it on your Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/github-issues.md): Add the prized label to a GitHub issue and the Claude Code or Codex on your Prized box works it and opens a pull request; a /prized comment on that pull request continues the same session. A small poller you run on the box, not a GitHub App.
- [Run coding agents from your own tools: plan, then implement](https://prized.dev/docs/orchestrate-coding-agents.md): Start Claude Code or Codex on a Prized box from a script, a Slack bot or an orchestrator over HTTP, one git worktree per plan item, and get a signed webhook when each run ends: a replacement for Conductor Cloud's API, with a Python example.
- [Run Superset's agents on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/superset.md): Add a Prized box as a Superset host, so Superset's parallel agents run on a cloud Linux machine that keeps working while your laptop sleeps, with a size for each number of agents.
- [Run Orca's agents on Prized](https://prized.dev/docs/orca.md): Run Orca's coding agents on Prized: the Prized Sandbox plugin gives each workspace its own sandbox that sleeps and wakes with it, or a Prized box as an SSH host keeps them running with your laptop closed.
- [Use opencode Desktop with a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/opencode-desktop.md): Add a Prized box as an SSH server in opencode Desktop, so its sessions run on a cloud Linux machine that keeps working while your laptop sleeps: what Desktop installs there, provider sign-ins on the box, and reconnecting.
- [Run T3 Code's agents remotely over SSH on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/t3-code.md): Add a Prized box as a remote SSH environment in T3 Code, so its Claude Code and Codex threads run on a cloud Linux machine and keep going with your laptop closed: what T3 Code installs there, the agent CLIs it expects, its browser on Ubuntu 24.04, and worktrees.
- [Use a Prized box with the herdr app](https://prized.dev/docs/herdr-app.md): Add a Prized box to the herdr app as an SSH machine, so its Claude Code and Codex run on a cloud Linux machine that keeps working while your laptop sleeps: what herdr installs on the box, putting the agents on its path, and what a paused box looks like.
- [Run Happy Agent on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/happy.md): Put Happy Agent on a Prized box as a remote server, so the Claude Code and Codex sessions you drive from Happy Desktop or your phone keep running while your laptop sleeps: what it installs, the sign-ins it needs, and keeping the box awake.
- [Run Paseo's agents on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/paseo.md): Run the Paseo daemon on a Prized box and add it as a Remote SSH host, so Paseo's parallel agents work in worktrees on a cloud Linux machine: installing the daemon, connecting, worktrees and ports, and when it is refused.
- [Run Vibe Kanban in the cloud on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/vibe-kanban.md): Run Vibe Kanban on a Prized box, a cloud Linux machine, and open it in your browser, so Claude Code and Codex work there in a git worktree per workspace and keep going with your laptop closed: Node.js on the box, the ports, sign-ins, and restarts.
- [Run Ouroboros on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/ouroboros.md): Install Ouroboros on a Prized box, so its interview, Seed and build loop drives Claude Code on a cloud Linux machine and keeps going with your laptop closed.
- [Run Hermes Agent on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/hermes-agent.md): Keep Hermes Agent and its messaging gateway running around the clock on a Prized box, so it answers on Telegram, Discord, Slack or WhatsApp with your laptop closed.
- [Run OpenClaw on a Prized box](https://prized.dev/docs/openclaw.md): Keep the OpenClaw Gateway running around the clock on a Prized box, so your assistant answers on WhatsApp, Telegram, Discord or Slack with your laptop closed, and open its Control UI on your localhost.
- [Run code from an AI agent in an isolated VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-run-agent-code.md): Run an AI agent's commands in a microVM of its own: create a sandbox, run each command with a timeout, move files in and out, and delete it, with what it costs.
- [Run an orchestrator agent with a sandbox per task](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-orchestrator.md): Keep the orchestrator agent on a box and give each builder or task its own sandbox, deleted when the task ends: a Python fan-out script, cleanup, costs, and limits.
- [Fork a running VM to run work in parallel](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-fork-parallel.md): Set up one sandbox, fork it into live copies with the setup done, run a different job in each, collect the results, and delete them all, with what it costs.
- [Fork a computer-use agent's desktop](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-fork-computer-use-desktop.md): Run Chrome on a Linux desktop in a sandbox, let Claude's computer use tool drive it, then fork the sandbox mid-task into live copies that carry on from the same screen.
- [Evals and RL harnesses: fork a warm environment for every rollout](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-evals-rl-harnesses.md): Set up an eval or RL environment once in a sandbox, with its server running and its models in memory, and fork a live copy of it for every rollout with the Python SDK: the script, its measured time and cost against a cold start, and the limits.
- [Run Claude Code with --dangerously-skip-permissions in an isolated VM](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-claude-code-skip-permissions.md): Let Claude Code or Codex run every command without asking, in a cloud microVM that holds only the repo and key you give it: create the sandbox, copy the project in, run the agent, take the diff out, and delete it, with what it costs.
- [AI agent sandbox that pauses with memory and runs past 24 hours](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-long-running.md): A sandbox for an AI agent that pauses with its memory and running processes, resumes on the next command, and, once the workspace has paid in, runs up to 7 days at a time, past E2B Pro's 24-hour sessions: the commands, the limits, and what it costs.
- [Run hosted Firecracker microVMs for AI agents through an API](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-firecracker-api.md): Firecracker microVMs your agent creates, runs commands in, pauses with their memory, forks and deletes through an HTTP API or the CLI, with no hosts, kernels or images of your own to run, billed per second.
- [Use a sandbox as your own template](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-own-template.md): Set up one sandbox with your packages, repo and system libraries, pause it, and start every job as a fork of it, with the install done.
- [Run Docker and Compose in a sandbox](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-docker.md): Run Docker, Compose and docker build in a Prized sandbox on a workspace that has paid in: create from prized-docker-v1, start your stack once, pause it, and fork it into live copies with the containers still running, one per job or RL episode.
- [Open in Prized](https://prized.dev/docs/open-in-prized.md): A link and README badge that opens a public GitHub repository on a Prized box, cloned into ~/code, with Claude Code or Codex started in it.
- [Moving from Ona (Gitpod)](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-ona.md): Use a Prized box as an Ona or Gitpod alternative now that Ona Cloud closed on October 31, 2026: save what you can still reach, turn .gitpod.yml, devcontainer.json, and .ona/config.yaml into a setup script, and run it on a box.
- [Moving from Jules](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-jules.md): Google's Jules runs sessions until November 11, 2026, then goes read-only, and shuts down on December 31. Export your Jules data, then hand Claude Code or Codex a task on a Prized box from your browser, close the tab, and get a pull request back.
- [Migrate from Conductor Cloud (read-only Nov 13, shuts down Nov 20)](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-conductor-cloud.md): Before Conductor Cloud goes read-only on November 13, 2026, save your cloud workspaces, then run Claude Code or Codex on your own Claude or ChatGPT subscription, a git worktree each, on a Prized box that keeps working with your laptop closed.
- [Run Conductor-style agent tasks from code](https://prized.dev/docs/conductor-api-agent-tasks.md): Your Slack bot or orchestrator created Conductor Cloud workspaces through its API or CLI. Keep the code and swap the calls: a sandbox per task from the Prized Python SDK, or a run in a git worktree on your box with a signed webhook when it ends.
- [Moving from Runloop](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-runloop.md): Use Prized sandboxes as a Runloop alternative: the Prized command for each Runloop devbox call, a short ported script, and what is not on Prized yet.
- [Moving from E2B](https://prized.dev/docs/moving-from-e2b.md): Run E2B Python and TypeScript code on Prized sandboxes by changing its imports (code that creates sandboxes, runs commands, moves files, pauses and forks), map each E2B call to the Prized API and CLI, and see what works differently and what is not on Prized yet.

## Connect

- [Sync](https://prized.dev/docs/sync.md): Mirror a project folder between the box and your machine, both directions and git history included, with edits landing on the other side in under a second.
- [Mount](https://prized.dev/docs/mount.md): Browse the whole box in Finder as a live network drive; nothing is copied and it uses no disk space on your Mac.
- [Ports](https://prized.dev/docs/ports.md): A server on the box opens on your machine as localhost within about two seconds, with no flags or config, and any port can be shared at a link with your workspace's members.
- [Sessions](https://prized.dev/docs/sessions.md): Your shell runs in a persistent session on the box, so a network drop or a closed lid ends only the connection; reconnect and you are where you left off, running processes included.
- [herdr](https://prized.dev/docs/herdr.md): Land in herdr instead of tmux, a terminal multiplexer that shows which of your coding agents is working, waiting on you, or done.
- [Run commands and files](https://prized.dev/docs/exec.md): Run a command on your box and get its exit code, leave one running and follow it live, read any command's output later even with the box paused (not open to every workspace yet), or copy files either way, from the CLI or any script holding an API key, with nothing to install on the box.
- [Desktop](https://prized.dev/docs/desktop.md): A full Linux desktop, or a single Chrome window, from any box in your browser, streamed over VNC inside the private tunnel with nothing opened to the internet.

## Agents

- [Agents](https://prized.dev/docs/agents.md): Start a coding agent on your box, close the laptop, and let it work; it is an ordinary process on a machine you already pay for, so nothing it does is metered.
- [Prompt an agent remotely](https://prized.dev/docs/remote-agents.md): Prompt Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode or pi on your box from your machine or any script, on the model and backend you pick, follow the run as it happens, and interrupt it; the run lives on the box, so nothing depends on your laptop staying open.
- [Codex remote](https://prized.dev/docs/codex.md): Run Codex on your box from the ChatGPT desktop app, with every edit syncing back to your Mac.
- [Claude Desktop remote](https://prized.dev/docs/claude-desktop.md): Run Claude Code on your box from the Claude desktop app's Code tab over SSH, with nothing to install on the box first, and what to know about sign-in, ports and long tasks.

## Boxes

- [Boxes](https://prized.dev/docs/boxes.md): Create, pause, resize, move, and delete boxes: dedicated Linux machines from Nano to Extra Large.
- [Machines](https://prized.dev/docs/machines.md): What a box is made of: its size and disk, Ubuntu 24.04 on a dedicated x86_64 virtual machine, preinstalled software, and the Prized processes beside your work.
- [GPU boxes](https://prized.dev/docs/gpu-boxes.md): Boxes with one NVIDIA GPU, an L4, billed by the hour from your prepaid balance. In beta.
- [Snapshots](https://prized.dev/docs/snapshots.md): Take point-in-time images of a box's disk by hand or on a schedule, restore them into new boxes, and fork a running box.
- [Environments](https://prized.dev/docs/environments.md): What a new box inherits, kept as versions: repos to clone, a setup script, env vars, secret files, and which of your credentials it may receive (CLI and API only).
- [Credentials](https://prized.dev/docs/credentials.md): How your agents' and tools' sign-ins reach a box: handed over from your machine through the SSH tunnel, never seen, stored, or proxied by the control plane.
- [Connectors](https://prized.dev/docs/connectors.md): Mount brokered credentials for ClickHouse, OpenAI, Anthropic, GitHub, or Slack into a dev box without writing the real value to its disk.
- [AWS access](https://prized.dev/docs/aws.md): Connect an IAM role once, and every box in your workspace gets an AWS profile that assumes it with a short-lived identity token instead of access keys. In early access.
- [Prized vs GitHub Codespaces, Coder and DevPod](https://prized.dev/docs/boxes-vs-codespaces-coder-devpod.md): How a Prized box compares with GitHub Codespaces, Coder and DevPod as a cloud dev environment: who runs it, devcontainer.json, how long it runs, files and ports, price and free use, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 3, 2026.
- [GitHub Codespaces alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/codespaces-alternatives.md): Five GitHub Codespaces alternatives for a cloud dev environment (Prized, Coder, DevPod, Google Cloud Workstations, and a VM of your own) with when each fits better and where Codespaces still wins, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 3, 2026.
- [Gitpod and Ona Cloud alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/gitpod-ona-alternatives.md): Where to take your environments now that Ona Cloud, formerly Gitpod, closed on October 31, 2026: Prized, Codex Cloud, GitHub Codespaces, Coder and DevPod, with when each fits better, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 3, 2026.
- [Google Jules alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/jules-alternatives.md): Google's Jules runs sessions until November 11, 2026, then goes read-only, and shuts down on December 31. Where Jules users are taking their async coding tasks (Google Antigravity's remote agents, GitHub Copilot's cloud agent, Claude Code on the web, Codex Cloud, Cursor Cloud Agents, Devin, OpenHands or a Prized box), with when each fits, from each vendor's own pages, checked November 5, 2026.
- [Conductor Cloud alternatives (shuts down Nov 20, 2026)](https://prized.dev/docs/conductor-cloud-alternatives.md): Conductor Cloud goes read-only on November 13, 2026 and shuts down on November 20. Where to run your coding agents next (Cloudroom, AQ, Aether, Claude Code on the web, Codex Cloud, Cursor Cloud Agents, Paradev, Emdash or Orca with a machine of your own, your own VM and tmux, or a Prized box), with when each fits, from each vendor's own pages, checked November 4, 2026.

## Sandboxes

- [Sandboxes](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes.md): Sandboxes (beta): Linux microVMs your code creates with the Python or TypeScript SDK, the API, or the CLI. Each boots from a template, runs commands and moves files, pauses for the price of its storage, and forks into many live copies.
- [Sandbox API](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-api.md): Every sandbox route: create, list, pause, resume, fork, delete, and SSH access on the control plane; run commands, read a background command's output as it grows, and move files on the edge; with the fields, states, and error codes.
- [Sandbox SDK for Python and TypeScript](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-python-typescript.md): Install the Python SDK (pip install prized) or the TypeScript SDK (npm i @prized/sdk) to create sandboxes from your code, run commands, pause them with their memory, and fork a running or paused sandbox into live copies with its memory and processes, on the same account and balance as your Prized boxes.
- [Use Prized from your coding agent](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-coding-agents.md): Teach Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or another coding agent to run code in Prized sandboxes: install the Prized skill, or paste a short section into AGENTS.md.
- [Run Vercel AI SDK tool calls in a sandbox](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-vercel-ai-sdk.md): Give a Vercel AI SDK agent a runCommand tool: each session gets its own sandbox, the model's commands run there through the Sandbox API, and the sandbox is deleted at the end.
- [Run a LangChain Deep Agent in a sandbox](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-langchain.md): The langchain-prized package is a sandbox backend for LangChain Deep Agents: the agent's shell commands and file tools run in a Prized sandbox, which is deleted when the agent is done.
- [Run an OpenAI Agents SDK agent in a sandbox](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-openai-agents.md): The openai-agents-prized package is a sandbox client for the OpenAI Agents SDK: a SandboxAgent's commands and file edits run in a Prized sandbox, which is deleted when the run ends.
- [Sandboxes FAQ](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-faq.md): Short answers about Sandboxes (beta): starting, forking, isolation, pricing, limits, what is not there yet, and where to get help.
- [Best cloud sandboxes for AI agents (2026)](https://prized.dev/docs/best-cloud-sandboxes-for-ai-agents.md): E2B, Daytona, Modal, boat and Prized compared on pause and resume with memory, fork, what persists, whether an agent can sign up by itself, regions and price, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 5, 2026.
- [Prized vs E2B, Daytona and Modal](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-vs-e2b-daytona-modal.md): How Prized sandboxes compare with E2B, Daytona and Modal on isolation, run length, pause and fork, price, free credit and GPUs, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 3, 2026.
- [Prized vs AWS Nitro Sandboxes and Lambda MicroVMs](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-vs-aws-nitro-sandboxes-lambda-microvms.md): How Prized sandboxes compare with AWS Nitro Sandboxes and AWS Lambda MicroVMs on what you get, where it runs, pause with memory, fork, lifetime and price, each AWS fact from AWS's or Firecracker's own pages, checked November 10, 2026.
- [E2B alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/e2b-alternatives.md): Six E2B alternatives for running agent and untrusted code in a sandbox (Prized, Daytona, Modal, Vercel Sandbox, Cloudflare Sandboxes and Sprites) with when each fits better and where E2B still wins, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 3, 2026.
- [Runloop alternatives](https://prized.dev/docs/runloop-alternatives.md): Runloop's transition support ends November 13, 2026. Six Runloop alternatives for agent devboxes (Prized, Tensorlake, Modal, Freestyle, Daytona and E2B) with when each fits better, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 3, 2026.
- [Daytona alternatives for AI agent sandboxes](https://prized.dev/docs/daytona-alternatives.md): Six Daytona alternatives for AI agent sandboxes (Prized, E2B, Modal, Vercel Sandbox, Freestyle and Sprites) with when each fits better and where Daytona still wins, each fact from the vendor's own pages, checked November 3, 2026.
- [Free sandbox APIs for AI agents (2026)](https://prized.dev/docs/free-sandbox-api.md): What each sandbox API gives AI agents for free, from each vendor's own pages: Prized's $30 of credit with no card (up to 10 sandboxes at a time, each up to 2 vCPU and 4 GB for 60 minutes) and a first sandbox in Python or TypeScript, then E2B, Daytona, Modal, Vercel Sandbox, Freestyle, CodeSandbox, Sprites, Blaxel and Tensorlake.
- [A sandbox your agent can sign up for itself (no browser, no card)](https://prized.dev/docs/sandboxes-agent-self-sign-up.md): Your coding agent can make your Prized account with no browser and no card, one call with your email address and one with the 6-digit code Prized emails you, then start a cloud sandbox. The steps it follows, and what you approve.

## Account

- [Dashboard](https://prized.dev/docs/dashboard.md): A map of the signed-in app at prized.dev/dashboard: every screen, its controls, and where each setting lives.
- [Workspaces](https://prized.dev/docs/workspaces.md): One account for the whole team; boxes, balance, and secrets shared by everyone in it.
- [Teams](https://prized.dev/docs/teams.md): Bring your team in by email or with one link, and what a shared workspace adds to one account: roles, policies that bind members, quiet hours, an audit log with a scheduled export, scoped secrets, and a live activity page.
- [Billing](https://prized.dev/docs/billing.md): How a monthly plan ($10 to $1,000, or sized to your boxes past that) becomes credit, what boxes cost by the hour, and what happens when the credit runs out.
- [Limits](https://prized.dev/docs/limits.md): Every hard cap a box or a workspace can hit, and what the refusal looks like.
- [Data retention](https://prized.dev/docs/data-retention.md): What pausing, deleting, and closing keep and remove, how zero data retention changes that, and what Prized retains afterwards.

## Reference

- [CLI reference](https://prized.dev/docs/cli.md): Every prized command, flag, JSON shape, and exit code, on macOS, Linux, and Windows.
- [Sign up from your terminal](https://prized.dev/docs/terminal-signup.md): One command signs you up, starts a box on the free credit, and sets up this machine: npx prized.
- [API reference](https://prized.dev/docs/api.md): Every HTTP route an API key can call: the control plane for boxes and workspace settings, the edge for commands, files, agent prompts, and stopping the desktop.
- [Troubleshooting](https://prized.dev/docs/troubleshooting.md): Symptoms and their fixes, starting with the one command that checks and repairs config, auth, tunnel, SSH, sync, the daemon, and the box agent.
- [Changelog](https://prized.dev/docs/changelog.md): What changed in each release of the prized CLI and box agent, and in the dashboard and the API, newest first.

## Trust

- [Security and trust](https://prized.dev/docs/security.md): How a box is reached, what the control plane can and cannot see, where credentials and secrets go, and how to report a problem.
- [Privacy](https://prized.dev/docs/privacy.md): What Prized records about how you use the product, and what it never records.

